POSTS=[
# ---------------- Condos and HOAs
dict(slug='what-a-condo-website-should-have',ind='Condos and HOAs',title='What a good condo website should have',
ex='Residents want answers in seconds. Here is what a building website needs so the front desk stops answering the same questions.',
body='''<p>Most condo websites are a logo and a PDF of the rules. A useful one saves your manager hours every week. Start with the questions residents ask most.</p>
<h3>Put the daily answers on the home page</h3>
<ul><li>Office hours, emergency numbers and who to call after hours</li><li>Trash, recycling and package pickup rules</li><li>Move-in and move-out steps, with the form right there</li><li>A short list of upcoming notices</li></ul>
<h3>Give residents one place to ask for things</h3>
<p>Amenity bookings, repair requests and gate or parking passes should each be a short form that goes to the right person. Email threads get lost when staff change. A form with a status keeps everyone on the same page.</p>
<h3>Keep documents easy to find</h3>
<p>Bylaws, meeting minutes, budgets and forms belong in a searchable library, grouped by year. Owners can find what they need without calling the office.</p>
<h3>Think about phones first</h3>
<p>Most residents will open it on their phone in the elevator. Big buttons, short forms and fast loading matter more than fancy design.</p>
<p>A portal like this is what we built for the Ardent Residences demo. You can click through it on our work page.</p>'''),
dict(slug='stop-taking-repair-requests-by-email',ind='Condos and HOAs',title='Why your building should stop taking repair requests by email',
ex='Email feels simple until a leak report is buried under forty messages. A tracked request form fixes that.',
body='''<p>A resident emails about a dripping ceiling. The manager is out. Two days later nobody can say who saw it. This happens in nearly every building that runs on inboxes.</p>
<h3>What goes wrong with email</h3>
<ul><li>Requests have no owner, so everyone assumes someone else has it</li><li>Details are missing: unit number, photos, what part of the room</li><li>Residents can’t see progress, so they send follow-ups</li><li>Boards can’t see patterns, like the same pipe failing every spring</li></ul>
<h3>What a better request form does</h3>
<p>It asks for the unit, a category, a description and a photo. It sends the request to the right person and gives the resident a status they can check: received, scheduled, done.</p>
<h3>What the board gets</h3>
<p>A simple dashboard shows open requests, how long they’ve been waiting and which categories come up most. That makes budget conversations easier because you have real numbers, not memory.</p>
<h3>Start small</h3>
<p>You don’t need a giant system. One form, one list for staff, and automatic emails to residents already removes most of the chaos.</p>'''),
# ---------------- Restaurants
dict(slug='what-every-restaurant-website-needs',ind='Restaurants',title='Seven things every restaurant website needs',
ex='Hungry people decide in under a minute. Make the important things impossible to miss.',
body='''<p>People look up a restaurant right before they go, usually on a phone. If they can’t find what they need fast, they pick the next place.</p>
<ul><li><b>Hours and address at the top.</b> Include a tap-to-call number and a tap-for-directions link.</li><li><b>A real text menu.</b> Not a PDF or a photo. Text loads fast, can be read by Google and is easy to update.</li><li><b>Prices on the menu.</b> Guests leave when they can’t tell what dinner will cost.</li><li><b>Photos of your actual food and room.</b> A few good ones beat a gallery of dull ones.</li><li><b>One clear button.</b> Reserve, order or both, in the same spot on every page.</li><li><b>Dietary notes.</b> Mark vegetarian, gluten-free and common allergens.</li><li><b>Fast loading on phones.</b> Heavy videos and huge images lose people on cellular.</li></ul>
<h3>Keep it current</h3>
<p>Holiday hours and sold-out dishes should be easy to change the same day. If updating the site takes a phone call to a developer, it won’t get done.</p>
<p>See how this fits together in our Brasa and Lime demo, which includes ordering, reservations and an owner dashboard.</p>'''),
dict(slug='online-ordering-on-your-own-site',ind='Restaurants',title='Online ordering on your own website vs delivery apps',
ex='Delivery apps bring orders, and they also take a cut. Here is how to think about using both.',
body='''<p>Delivery apps can bring new customers, but they usually charge a commission on every order. Orders placed on your own website keep more of each sale and give you the customer’s contact details.</p>
<h3>How to think about it</h3>
<ul><li><b>Apps are for discovery.</b> New people find you there.</li><li><b>Your site is for regulars.</b> People who already like you should order directly.</li></ul>
<h3>Nudge regulars to order direct</h3>
<ul><li>Put "Order direct" on your menu, receipts and Instagram bio</li><li>Offer something small for direct orders, like a free drink or a loyalty stamp</li><li>Make ordering take under a minute, with saved details for return guests</li></ul>
<h3>What your own ordering needs</h3>
<p>A clear menu with photos, pickup times that match your kitchen, tips, receipts by email or text and an order screen your staff can handle during a rush. Mark dishes sold out in one tap.</p>
<h3>Check the numbers</h3>
<p>Look at what you pay in fees each month across the apps. If it is a large part of your margin, a direct ordering page can pay for itself quickly.</p>'''),
# ---------------- Construction
dict(slug='win-more-bids-with-your-website',ind='Construction',title='How contractors can win more bids with their website',
ex='Homeowners and property managers judge your work by your site before they call. Show proof and make it easy to reach you.',
body='''<p>Most people hire a contractor after checking two or three. A clear site puts you on the short list.</p>
<h3>Show finished work</h3>
<p>Use real before and after photos with a sentence on each: what the job was, where and how long it took. Ten strong projects beat fifty random photos.</p>
<h3>Say what you do and where</h3>
<p>List your trades, service area and license number if your state requires it. Insurance and licensing build trust quickly.</p>
<h3>Make the bid request short</h3>
<ul><li>Name, phone, email</li><li>Type of project and address</li><li>Rough timeline and budget</li><li>A place to upload photos or plans</li></ul>
<p>Every extra field loses some people. Ask for the basics and follow up by phone.</p>
<h3>Reply quickly</h3>
<p>The first contractor to answer often gets the call. Send automatic text and email confirmations so people know you got it, then follow up the same day.</p>
<h3>Add reviews</h3>
<p>Quotes from past clients, with names and the type of job, are worth more than any slogan.</p>'''),
dict(slug='client-portal-for-construction-projects',ind='Construction',title='What to put in a client portal for construction projects',
ex='Clients call when they don’t know what’s happening. A simple private page answers most of those calls before they start.',
body='''<p>Long projects create lots of questions: what happened this week, what’s next, what do I owe. A client portal puts the answers in one private page.</p>
<h3>The basics that matter</h3>
<ul><li><b>Schedule.</b> Key dates and milestones, updated as things change</li><li><b>Photos.</b> A weekly set of progress photos with short captions</li><li><b>Documents.</b> Contracts, permits, change orders and warranties in one place</li><li><b>Invoices.</b> What’s due, what’s paid and how to pay</li><li><b>Messages.</b> One thread per project instead of scattered texts</li></ul>
<h3>Change orders</h3>
<p>Let clients approve changes in the portal so there is a record of what was agreed and the new price.</p>
<h3>Why it pays off</h3>
<p>Fewer interruptions on site, fewer arguments about what was promised and faster payments. Clients also feel taken care of, which leads to referrals.</p>
<p>Start simple with photos, documents and invoices, and add more once your team is used to it.</p>'''),
# ---------------- Real estate
dict(slug='what-makes-a-listing-page-convert',ind='Real estate',title='What makes a listing page turn visitors into leads',
ex='Buyers scroll fast. Great photos, honest details and one easy way to ask a question do most of the work.',
body='''<p>A listing page has one job: get a serious buyer to contact you. These things help most.</p>
<h3>Lead with the best photos</h3>
<p>The first image sets the mood. Use bright, straight photos and put the strongest room first. Add a floor plan if you have one.</p>
<h3>Put key facts above the fold</h3>
<ul><li>Price, beds, baths and square footage</li><li>Neighborhood and a map</li><li>HOA fees, parking and pet rules for condos</li></ul>
<h3>One clear action</h3>
<p>Offer a short form: name, phone and a choice of "Ask a question" or "Book a tour." Show open tour times so people can pick one in two taps.</p>
<h3>Make it fast on phones</h3>
<p>Many buyers browse on their phones, often on cellular. Compress images, load them as people scroll and keep the page light.</p>
<h3>Show the agent</h3>
<p>A photo, a short bio and recent sales make buyers feel they are contacting a person, not a form.</p>'''),
dict(slug='answer-every-real-estate-lead-fast',ind='Real estate',title='How to answer every real estate lead within minutes',
ex='Leads go cold quickly. A little automation makes sure none slips through.',
body='''<p>Someone who fills out a form is looking at several homes at once. The agent who replies first usually has the advantage.</p>
<h3>Route leads automatically</h3>
<p>Send each inquiry to the right agent based on the listing, area or price range. Include the property link and the buyer’s message in the alert.</p>
<h3>Reply instantly, then personally</h3>
<p>An automatic text or email confirms you got it and offers tour times. Then the agent follows up with a real message or call.</p>
<h3>Track every lead</h3>
<p>Keep a simple list of new, contacted, touring and closed. It shows which listings bring interest and which leads need a nudge.</p>
<h3>Make follow-ups easy</h3>
<ul><li>Reminders for leads you haven’t contacted in a few days</li><li>Saved replies for common questions</li><li>A link to book a tour in one tap</li></ul>
<p>None of this needs a huge system. A good form, instant alerts and a tidy list are enough to start.</p>'''),
# ---------------- Medical and wellness
dict(slug='online-booking-for-clinics-and-studios',ind='Medical and wellness',title='Online booking for clinics and wellness studios: what to get right',
ex='Patients and clients want to book at night. A good booking page fills your schedule and cuts phone calls.',
body='''<p>Many appointments are booked outside office hours. If people can only call, some will book somewhere else.</p>
<h3>Keep the booking steps short</h3>
<ul><li>Pick a service or provider</li><li>Pick a time that is actually open</li><li>Add name, phone and email</li><li>Get a confirmation right away</li></ul>
<h3>Reduce no-shows</h3>
<p>Text and email reminders, with a one-tap way to confirm or reschedule, free up empty slots. State your cancellation policy clearly before they book.</p>
<h3>Explain each service</h3>
<p>A line on what to expect, how long it takes and what it costs saves questions. New patients especially want to know what happens on the first visit.</p>
<h3>Make it readable</h3>
<p>Use large text, clear buttons and plain language. Many visitors are stressed or unwell, and some are older and not used to apps.</p>
<h3>Be careful with health details</h3>
<p>Do not ask for medical details in an ordinary booking form. Use tools built for healthcare where needed and check the rules that apply to your practice.</p>'''),
dict(slug='online-intake-forms-keep-information-safe',ind='Medical and wellness',title='Online intake forms: keep patient information safe',
ex='Digital forms save time at the front desk. They also need more care than a regular contact form.',
body='''<p>Paper forms slow down check-in and create re-typing mistakes. Online forms fix that, but health information needs extra protection.</p>
<h3>Use the right tools</h3>
<p>A normal website form or email is usually not the right place for sensitive health details. Look for software designed for healthcare and ask the vendor how they handle privacy. If you are in the US, ask whether they support the requirements that apply to your practice.</p>
<h3>Collect only what you need</h3>
<p>Ask for basics before the visit, like contact details and insurance. Gather detailed history through a secure system or at the appointment.</p>
<h3>Make forms easy</h3>
<ul><li>Let people save and finish later</li><li>Work well on phones</li><li>Use plain words, not medical jargon</li><li>Send a link by text or email before the visit</li></ul>
<h3>Tell people what happens to their data</h3>
<p>A short, clear privacy notice builds trust. Link to it near every form.</p>
<p>This is general information, not legal advice. Check the rules for your practice and ask a professional if you are unsure.</p>'''),
# ---------------- Law and finance
dict(slug='what-a-law-firm-website-should-say-first',ind='Law and finance',title='What a law or finance website should say in the first ten seconds',
ex='New clients are nervous and comparing options. Clear words and visible credentials help them pick up the phone.',
body='''<p>People searching for a lawyer or advisor are usually dealing with something stressful. Your site should calm them down and make the next step obvious.</p>
<h3>Say who you help and with what</h3>
<p>"Estate planning for families in Miami" works better than "Full-service legal solutions." Be specific about practice areas and location.</p>
<h3>Show credentials plainly</h3>
<p>Include licenses, years of experience and any professional memberships. Add a real photo of each attorney or advisor.</p>
<h3>Make contact simple</h3>
<ul><li>A phone number that works by tapping on mobile</li><li>A short form that doesn’t ask for case details</li><li>A line like "We reply within one business day"</li></ul>
<h3>Explain how it works</h3>
<p>A short "What happens next" section, such as a free first call, a plan and then a written fee agreement, removes fear of the unknown.</p>
<h3>Check the rules</h3>
<p>Advertising rules for lawyers and financial professionals vary by state and regulator. Check yours before publishing testimonials, results or claims. This is general information, not legal advice.</p>'''),
dict(slug='client-intake-that-protects-confidentiality',ind='Law and finance',title='Client intake forms that protect confidentiality',
ex='A good intake form saves your team time. It should also avoid collecting sensitive details too early.',
body='''<p>First contact is where many firms make avoidable mistakes. A website form is not the same as a private conversation.</p>
<h3>Keep the first form light</h3>
<p>Ask for name, contact details, the general type of matter and the best time to call. Add a short notice asking people not to include confidential details until you have confirmed a working relationship.</p>
<h3>Add a clear notice</h3>
<p>Explain that sending a form does not create a client relationship. Your attorney or compliance advisor can give you the right wording for your state.</p>
<h3>Use secure channels for the details</h3>
<ul><li>A secure client portal for documents</li><li>Encrypted messaging where available</li><li>Avoid asking clients to email sensitive files</li></ul>
<h3>Route and track</h3>
<p>Send each inquiry to the right person and log it, so nothing is missed and you can follow up on time.</p>
<p>Rules differ by profession and place. Check with your regulator or counsel. This article is general information only.</p>'''),
# ---------------- Retail
dict(slug='product-pages-that-sell',ind='Retail',title='Product pages that sell: photos, words and trust',
ex='Shoppers can’t touch your product. Your page has to do that job.',
body='''<p>On a product page, people look for reasons to buy and reasons to leave. Remove the second group.</p>
<h3>Photos first</h3>
<p>Show the product from several angles, in use, and next to something that shows size. Good natural light beats expensive equipment.</p>
<h3>Write for questions</h3>
<ul><li>What is it made of?</li><li>What size or color options are there?</li><li>How long does shipping take?</li><li>Can I return it?</li></ul>
<p>Answer these on the page, in plain words, near the buy button.</p>
<h3>Show the price and shipping early</h3>
<p>Surprise costs at checkout are one of the biggest reasons people abandon a cart. Say what shipping costs before they get that far.</p>
<h3>Build trust</h3>
<p>Real reviews, a clear return policy and a visible way to contact you reassure first-time buyers.</p>
<h3>Keep checkout short</h3>
<p>Offer guest checkout, saved details for returning customers and common payment methods like Apple Pay.</p>
<p>Our Sunday Bean demo shows a small shop with product pop-ups, a cart and a simple checkout.</p>'''),
dict(slug='subscriptions-for-small-shops',ind='Retail',title='Subscriptions for small shops: when they make sense',
ex='If people reorder your products, a subscription can give you steadier sales and give them less to remember.',
body='''<p>A subscription works best when customers already buy the same thing again and again, like coffee, snacks, skincare or pet supplies.</p>
<h3>Good signs</h3>
<ul><li>Customers reorder regularly</li><li>Your product gets used up</li><li>People forget to reorder and run out</li></ul>
<h3>Make it easy and fair</h3>
<ul><li>Let customers choose how often: weekly, every two weeks or monthly</li><li>Offer a modest discount for subscribing</li><li>Allow skipping, pausing and cancelling in a couple of taps</li><li>Send a reminder before each shipment</li></ul>
<p>Easy cancelling builds trust and actually makes people more willing to sign up.</p>
<h3>Show the numbers to the owner</h3>
<p>A back office should show active subscribers, monthly recurring revenue and who skipped or cancelled and when, so you can plan roasting, baking or stock.</p>
<h3>Start with one product</h3>
<p>Test with your best-seller first, learn how customers use it and then expand.</p>'''),
# ---------------- Hospitality
dict(slug='get-more-direct-bookings',ind='Hospitality',title='Direct bookings: why your own website should be the easiest place to book',
ex='Booking sites bring guests, and they charge for it. Your site can win repeat guests at a better margin.',
body='''<p>Many hotels and rentals pay a commission on every booking made through third-party sites. A direct booking skips that fee and gives you the guest’s contact details.</p>
<h3>Make direct feel better</h3>
<ul><li>Show availability and the full price clearly</li><li>Offer a small perk for booking direct, such as late checkout or a welcome drink</li><li>Keep the booking form short</li></ul>
<h3>Show your property well</h3>
<p>Good photos of every room type, the neighborhood and shared spaces help guests feel confident. Add a short list of what is nearby.</p>
<h3>Remove doubt</h3>
<p>State cancellation rules, check-in times and parking in plain words. Add real guest reviews.</p>
<h3>Stay in touch</h3>
<p>Ask for email at booking and send a short note before arrival and a thank-you after. Past guests are your cheapest future bookings.</p>
<h3>Make it work on phones</h3>
<p>Most guests browse and book from a phone. Big buttons and a fast page matter.</p>'''),
dict(slug='guest-guides-that-live-in-their-pocket',ind='Hospitality',title='Guest guides that live in your guests’ pocket',
ex='A simple mobile page replaces the binder in the room and answers questions before guests ask.',
body='''<p>Binders get lost and printed sheets go out of date. A mobile guide is always current and always with the guest.</p>
<h3>What to include</h3>
<ul><li>Wi-Fi name and password, with a tap-to-copy button</li><li>Check-in and check-out details</li><li>How things work: parking, air conditioning, appliances</li><li>Local recommendations, grouped by food, coffee, beaches and things to do</li><li>Contact numbers for help</li></ul>
<h3>Share it easily</h3>
<p>Send a link by text or email before arrival and place a QR code in the room. It should work without installing an app.</p>
<h3>Keep it short and visual</h3>
<p>Short sections with photos beat long paragraphs. Put the answers to the three most common questions at the top.</p>
<h3>Use it for events too</h3>
<p>For weddings and events, the same page can hold the schedule, map, shuttle times and contact numbers for the host.</p>
<p>Update it in minutes when something changes, so guests always see the latest.</p>'''),
]
